## Basic Nmap Scan

Now we perform our first scan.

`nmap -sT -p 8000 localhost`

Let's break down this command:

*   `-sT` is an option that specifies a TCP connect scan. This tells Nmap to use the TCP connect method to check the status of the ports.
    
*   `-p 8000` indicates that we want Nmap to scan only port 8000. You can change this number to scan other ports if needed.
    
*   `localhost` is the target of our scan. It refers to the local machine where the service is running.
    

Output example:

`PORT STATE SERVICE 8000/tcp open http-alt`

This means:

*   Port 8000 is open
    
*   A web service is running
    

> While I used TCP connect scan (-sT) in this lab environment, I learned that in real penetration testing scenarios, stealth SYN scans (-sS) are often preferred because they are less detectable.

**Technical Depth :**

A <mark class="bg-yellow-200 dark:bg-yellow-500/30">TCP connect scan (-sT)</mark> completes the full three-way handshake (SYN → SYN/ACK → ACK) before closing the connection.  
In contrast, a <mark class="bg-yellow-200 dark:bg-yellow-500/30">SYN scan (-sS) </mark> sends only the initial SYN packet and analyzes the response, making it faster and often less detectable.  
Understanding this difference helped me realize that scanning is not just about commands — it’s about how TCP/IP communication actually works under the hood.

## Service Version Detection

Sometimes, just knowing that a port is open isn't enough. We might want to know more about the service running on that port, such as its version.

To identify service details:

`nmap -sV -p 8000 localhost`

The `-sV` option is used to tell Nmap to probe open ports to determine service/version information. This means Nmap will try to figure out what specific software and version is running on the open port.

Output:

`PORT STATE SERVICE VERSION 8000/tcp open http SimpleHTTPServer 0.6`

This reveals:

*   Service type
    
*   Software name
    
*   Version information
    

This is extremely useful in vulnerability assessment.

## Running My First Nmap Scan

### Scan common 1000 ports

Now came the most exciting part — performing my first scan.

I ran:

`nmap localhost`

This command instructed Nmap to scan my own system and identify open ports.

When the scan completed, Nmap displayed a list of ports and their states.

Each line revealed something important:

*   Port number
    
*   Whether it was open, closed, or filtered
    
*   The service associated with the port
    

This was fascinating.

Even though my system looked quiet on the surface, Nmap was able to see deeper into its network behavior.

It revealed the hidden communication points of the system.

This is exactly how cybersecurity professionals discover exposed services.

### Scan all ports

`nmap -p- localhost`

This scans all **65535 ports**, providing complete visibility.

### Scan specific port range

`nmap -p 1-1000 localhost`

Useful when focusing on specific services.

